WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.254
"The Pros and Cons of Testing PSA Levels
for Prostate Cancer"

00:00:07.317 --> 00:00:10.740
While 64 percent of men
develop hidden prostate cancers

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.700
by their 60s,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.060
the lifetime risk of being
diagnosed with prostate cancer

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.530
is only about 11 percent,

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.320
and the risk of dying from
prostate cancer, 2.5 percent,

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.780
at the average age of 80.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.260
So most men develop
prostate cancer

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.720
but they die with their tumors

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.160
rather than from their tumors.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.340
Most men with prostate cancer
live their whole lives

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.740
never knowing they even had it.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.320
That's one of the problems
screening for it.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.900
Many prostate cancers
that are detected

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.300
may never have led to harm

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.640
even if they'd gone
undiscovered.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.000
Nonetheless,
not all men are so lucky.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.200
About 30,000 Americans die
each year from prostate cancer.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.760
So, should you get a PSA
prostate screening test or not?

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.540
PSA stands for
prostate specific antigen,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.420
an enzyme secreted
by cells of the prostate

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.820
to liquefy semen
and cervical mucus

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.320
to facilitate fertilization.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.040
Elevated levels in the blood can
be a sign of prostate cancer,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.400
which led to FDA approval
as a screening test

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.220
for the early detection
of prostate cancer in the 1990s.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.120
If it comes back high,
the test is usually repeated.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.500
If it's still high,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.120
the next step is typically

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.640
an ultrasound-guided
biopsy of the prostate

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.540
through the rectum.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:28.980
If there's cancer,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.720
then options include surgery,
radiation, and chemotherapy,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.280
or a delayal of treatment.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.100
However the USPSTF,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.160
the US Preventive Services
Task Force,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.620
the main independent
scientific panel

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.380
that sets evidence-based
clinical prevention guidelines,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.820
recommended against
routine PSA screening,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.880
as does the American College
of Preventive Medicine,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.400
the American Academy
of Family Physicians,

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.020
and the vast majority,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.840
85 percent of professional
medical societies

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.080
in developed countries
around the world, opposed.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.780
In 2018, though,

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:11.100
the USPSTF shifted from
a summary judgement against

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.180
to, quote, "the decision about
whether to be screened

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.300
for prostate cancer should
be an individual one," unquote,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.320
which is more in line with the
"shared decision making" stance

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.420
of the American
Urological Association,

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.160
the American College
of Physicians,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.260
and the American Cancer Society.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.160
In other words,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.560
men should be informed
about the risks and benefits

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.060
and decide for themselves.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.060
However,
men who are on the fence

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.280
and don't express
a clear preference

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.800
in favor of screening
should not be screened,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.140
according to the latest
USPSTF recommendations.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.640
More recently, an international
panel of experts concluded

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.920
that clinicians
need not feel obligated

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.540
to systematically bring it up,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:57.360
judging that most men would
decide to decline PSA testing,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.480
given the clear harms and
small and uncertain benefits.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.480
That, however, is up to you.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.080
I personally declined,
but let's run the numbers.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.860
Similar to the 92 percent
of women who didn't know

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.860
or overestimated the mortality
reduction from mammograms

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.880
by tenfold or more,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.600
89 percent of men vastly
overestimated the benefits

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:19.740
of prostate cancer screening

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.180
or simply had no idea.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.500
Most thought 50 prostate cancer
deaths could be prevented

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.600
out of a thousand men
regularly screened,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.640
when in reality,
it's more like one.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.020
But doesn't even a 1-in-1,000
chance of not dying from cancer

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:35.760
make a few blood tests worth it?

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.080
The downsides are more
than inconvenience, though.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.260
About one in seven men
who undergo PSA screening

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.160
will test positive,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.500
yet in two-thirds of the cases,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.060
the biopsy results
will be normal.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.360
So out of the thousand men
regularly screened,

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.820
about 150 will have
a false alarm

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.860
and be biopsied unnecessarily,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.780
which can cause
minor complications

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.060
like pain and bloody ejaculate,

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.400
or in approximately
1 percent of cases,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.780
more serious complications,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.420
like blood-borne infections
that require hospitalization.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.560
The greatest harm, though,
is overdiagnosis.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.960
Unnecessary biopsies
are bad enough,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.920
but nothing, compared to
unnecessary cancer treatment.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.720
Large-scale randomized trials
suggest that

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.420
20 to 50 percent of men
diagnosed with prostate cancer

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:28.380
would have never become
symptomatic in their lifetime.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.080
They never would
have been any wiser

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:32.220
had they not been screened,

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.580
but now they may be needlessly
heading to the operating table.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.940
About 3 in 1,000 men die
during or soon after

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.260
radical prostatectomy.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.720
That may help explain
why there appears to be

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.340
no overall mortality benefit
to prostate cancer screening.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.080
For every life that is saved,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.020
another may be extinguished
for a cancer

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.940
they never would have even
known about.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.220
Another 50 in 1,000 men

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.560
end up with serious
surgical complications.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.140
Even if the surgery
goes smoothly,

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.560
about one in five men develop
long-term urinary incontinence

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.360
requiring the use of pads,

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.340
and most men--
two out of three--

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.920
will experience long-term
erectile dysfunction.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.160
Most men who receive
radiation therapy

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.710
also experience long-term
sexual erectile dysfunction

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.160
and up to one in six experience
long-term bowel issues

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.600
such as fecal incontinence.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.080
If this was saving your life,
it would be worth it,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.700
but over 16 years,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.640
rather than being saved
from a prostate cancer death

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.360
by screening,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.540
it may be 25 times more likely
that you were instead

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.600
overdiagnosed with a cancer
that wouldn't have bothered you.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.460
Yet you come away
after treatment

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.520
thinking the PSA test
saved your life.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.260
It's like with the mammograms.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.480
The people who are
harmed the most--

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.040
unnecessary cancer treatment--

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.580
feel as though
they've been helped the most.

